.Dd September 29, 2026
.Dt KVM-DELETE 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kvm-delete
.Nd remove a virtual machine and everything it owns
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Ar name
.Op Fl -force
.Nm
.Fl h | -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
removes the virtual machine
.Ar name
and what belongs to it alone:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
its libvirt definition and its UEFI variable store;
.It
its disk,
.Pa rpool/vms/ Ns Ar name ,
with every snapshot of it;
.It
its data disk, a volume called
.Ar name Ns Pa -data
attached to it, wherever it lives;
.It
the cloud-init seed
.Xr kvm-clone 8
gave it,
.Ar name Ns Pa -seed.iso ;
.It
the snapshots
.Xr kvm-clone 8
took of its source, once no clone uses them;
.It
its row in the kldload state database, so the inventory stops listing it;
.It
its WireGuard mesh peer, if it was enrolled.
.El
.Pp
Anything else attached to it \(em an installer image, a disk shared with
another machine \(em is left alone.
A UEFI variable store that another machine also points at is kept; clones
made before 2026-09-29 share their source's.
.Pp
Everything that can refuse is asked before anything is removed.
.Nm
changes nothing when:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
a machine was cloned from this one, or from its data disk: removing the
disk would strand them, and ZFS would refuse anyway;
.It
the machine is not shut off and
.Fl -force
was not given;
.It
the power-off, or libvirt's undefine, fails;
.It
there is no machine of that name at all.
.El
.Pp
A machine with a disk and no libvirt definition, left by an earlier
failure, is removed with a warning; so is a definition with no disk.
.Pp
A disk that ZFS reports busy, as a volume often is in the seconds after
its machine stopped, is tried five times, two seconds apart.
If it still cannot be destroyed, the definition is already gone:
.Nm
says so, clears the database row anyway, and exits 1.
.Pp
Names are letters, digits,
.Ql \&. ,
.Ql _
and
.Ql - ,
up to 63 characters, starting with a letter or a digit.
.Pp
.Nm
must run as root; run as a user it re-executes itself with
.Xr sudo 8 .
.Fl -help
needs neither.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -force
Power the machine off first, as pulling its plug would, if it is running,
paused, or in any state but shut off.
Nothing in its memory is saved first.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print the usage and exit 0.
Needs no root and changes nothing.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa rpool/vms/ Ns Ar name
The machine's disk.
.It Ar pool Ns Pa / Ns Ar ... Ns Pa / Ns Ar name Ns Pa -data
Its data disk, found through its libvirt definition; with no definition,
.Pa rpool/vms/ Ns Ar name Ns Pa -data
only.
.It Pa /var/lib/libvirt/qemu/nvram/ Ns Ar name Ns Pa _VARS Ns \&. Ns Ar ext
Its UEFI variables.
.It Pa /var/lib/libvirt/images/ Ns Ar name Ns Pa -seed.iso
Its cloud-init seed.
.It Pa /var/lib/kldload/mesh/enrolled/ Ns Ar name
Its mesh enrolment, for machines enrolled before the state database
recorded it.
.It Pa /etc/wireguard/wg-mgmt.conf
The mesh configuration its peer is removed from.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width 1n -compact
.It 0
The machine and everything it owned are gone.
.It 1
Nothing was changed, for one of the reasons above; or the definition was
removed but a disk is still on disk or the machine is still a mesh peer,
and the warnings say which.
.It 2
A usage error: no name, a name that is not a plain name, or an argument
other than
.Fl -force .
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Every output below was printed by
.Nm
on a kldload host on 2026-09-29, against throwaway machines made for the
purpose with
.Xr kvm-create 8
and
.Xr kvm-clone 8 :
.Ql dl-3234727-src ,
a clone of it, and machines left running, paused, and with a disk but no
definition.
.Ss A machine that is shut off
The clone was defined and never started.
Its seed went with it, and so did the snapshot of its source that it was
made from, which nothing else used:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kvm-delete dl-3234727-clone
Removing VM definition...
Destroying ZFS zvol: rpool/vms/dl-3234727-clone
Cleaning up orphaned snapshot: rpool/vms/dl-3234727-src@clone-20260929_224555465177330

Deleted 'dl-3234727-clone'
# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Ss A running machine (--force)
Without
.Fl -force
a running or paused machine is refused:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kvm-delete dl-3234727-running
kvm-delete: dl-3234727-running is running; shut it down first or use --force
# (exited 1)
# kvm-delete dl-3234727-paused
kvm-delete: dl-3234727-paused is paused; shut it down first or use --force
# (exited 1)
.Ed
.Pp
With it, the machine is powered off first.
This one had a data disk attached, which went with it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kvm-delete dl-3234727-running --force
Force-stopping VM (running)...
Removing VM definition...
Destroying ZFS zvol: rpool/vms/dl-3234727-running
Destroying its data disk: rpool/vms/dl-3234727-running-data

Deleted 'dl-3234727-running'
# (exited 0)
# kvm-delete dl-3234727-paused --force
Force-stopping VM (paused)...
Removing VM definition...
Destroying ZFS zvol: rpool/vms/dl-3234727-paused

Deleted 'dl-3234727-paused'
# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Ss A source with clones
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kvm-delete dl-3234727-src
ERROR: dl-3234727-src still has machines cloned from it:
  rpool/vms/dl-3234727-clone

Nothing has been changed. Delete those first, or take one off its origin
with:

  zfs promote <clone>

which makes that clone own the data and leaves the rest working. Deleting
this VM with its clones still attached would strand every one of them.
# (exited 1)
.Ed
.Pp
Once the clone was deleted:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kvm-delete dl-3234727-src --force
Force-stopping VM (running)...
Removing VM definition...
Destroying ZFS zvol: rpool/vms/dl-3234727-src

Deleted 'dl-3234727-src'
# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Ss A disk with no definition
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kvm-delete dl-3234727-zonly
Warning: VM 'dl-3234727-zonly' not found in libvirt (removing zvol only)
Destroying ZFS zvol: rpool/vms/dl-3234727-zonly

Deleted 'dl-3234727-zonly'
# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Ss What it refuses
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kvm-delete nosuch-dl-3234727
kvm-delete: no VM named nosuch-dl-3234727 (no libvirt domain, no rpool/vms/nosuch-dl-3234727)
# (exited 1)
$ kvm-delete web/data
kvm-delete: 'web/data' is not a plain name (letters, digits, . _ -)
# (exited 2)
$ kvm-delete x --froce
kvm-delete: unexpected argument(s): --froce
Usage: kvm-delete <vm-name> [--force]
\&...
# (exited 2)
.Ed
.Ss An enrolled machine
A machine enrolled on the WireGuard mesh with
.Xr kldload-enroll 8
is taken off it as well; nothing was enrolled on the host these examples
were captured on:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kvm-delete web01
# among its lines: Removed from the mesh (peer N, identity from state DB)
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -diag
.It "ERROR: NAME still has machines cloned from it:"
Followed by the clones.
Nothing was changed.
Delete them first, or make one independent with
.Xr zfs-promote 8 .
.It "ERROR: NAME's data disk DATASET has clones:"
The same, for the data disk.
.It "kvm-delete: NAME is STATE; shut it down first or use --force"
The machine is running, paused, or otherwise not shut off.
.It "kvm-delete: could not power NAME off; nothing has been removed"
libvirt refused the power-off; its own error is above.
.It "kvm-delete: libvirt would not undefine NAME; its disks are untouched"
The definition could not be removed, so neither was anything else.
.It "WARNING: could not destroy DATASET after 5 attempts: REASON"
The definition is gone and the disk is not.
The next lines give the command to finish by hand; the exit status is 1.
.It "keeping PATH: OTHER uses it too"
The UEFI variable store is shared with
.Ar OTHER ,
a clone made before 2026-09-29 or its source, and is left for it.
.It "WARNING: NAME is still a mesh peer (id N) \(em remove it: kube-network remove-peer N"
The peer could not be removed; the exit status is 1.
.It "WARNING: NAME's key is on the mesh but not under id N \(em left alone, check 'wg show'"
The identity on record no longer matches the peer that holds that id,
which can happen when an id was given to a newer machine.
Nothing is removed rather than risk cutting off the wrong one.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kld 1 ,
.Xr virsh 1 ,
.Xr kldload-db 8 ,
.Xr kldload-enroll 8 ,
.Xr kube-network 8 ,
.Xr kvm-clone 8 ,
.Xr kvm-create 8 ,
.Xr kvm-snap 8 ,
.Xr zfs-destroy 8 ,
.Xr zfs-promote 8
.Sh HISTORY
The refusal before any change dates from a delete that undefined the
machine and then failed to destroy its disk, leaving the disk stranded
and the operator believing the delete had failed.
The bounded retry dates from 2026-09-21, when a disk still held open for a
moment after its machine stopped left 4-k8s with a machine gone from
libvirt, its disk on disk, and its row still in the inventory.
Mesh removal was added on 2026-09-22, after an enrolled clone's peer was
found outliving it; the data disk and the seed on 2026-09-29, after both
were found outliving theirs.
.Pp
On 2026-09-29, writing this page: a name was not checked before it
reached
.Xr zfs-destroy 8 ;
a name that existed nowhere was reported deleted;
a paused machine counted as stopped;
and a failed power-off or undefine was ignored and the disk destroyed
under a definition that still existed.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An kldload Aq Mt anthony@kldload.com
.Sh CAVEATS
There is no confirmation and no undo.
A snapshot of the disk does not survive: the disk is destroyed with all
of its snapshots.
To keep a machine's state, send it somewhere first with
.Xr zfs-send 8 ,
or clone it with
.Xr kvm-clone 8
and promote the clone with
.Xr zfs-promote 8 .
.Pp
The snapshot a clone came from is removed only if it is named
.Ql clone- Ns Ar timestamp ,
as
.Xr kvm-clone 8
names the ones it takes; one taken by hand and given with
.Fl -snap
is kept.
